// 1. CLARIFY AND CONSTRAIN:
// 'How many URLs will be created per day? How long do
//  short URLs need to work? Do we need analytics?'
// Assumptions: 100M URLs/day created, 10B reads/day,
//              URLs active for 5 years, analytics: yes

// 2. ESTIMATE SCALE:
// - Create: 100M/day = 1,160/second
// - Read:   10B/day  = 115,740/second  <- this is the real load
// - Storage: 100M URLs * 5 years * 365 days = 182.5B total URLs
//            at 500 bytes each = ~91 TB
// - Read:write ratio = ~100:1 (very read-heavy)

// 3. SIMPLE VERSION (explain whiteboard sketch):
// Client -> API Server -> [shortCode generator] -> PostgreSQL
// GET /{code} -> lookup in DB -> 301 redirect to original URL
// Shortcode: 6-char base62 = 62^6 = ~56B unique codes (sufficient)

// 4. IDENTIFY BOTTLENECKS:
// - PostgreSQL can handle ~10K QPS
// - At 115K reads/second, DB is the bottleneck
// - BUT: reads are the same URLs repeatedly (popular links)
//   -> Cache hit rate likely very high for top URLs
//   -> URLs are immutable after creation (perfect cache candidates)

// 5. SCALE BOTTLENECKS:
// Add Redis cache in front of DB for reads:
// GET /{code} -> Redis (cache hit ~95%) -> DB (cache miss only)
// -> Effective DB load: 115K * 0.05 = 5,750/second (within capacity)

// For 10B reads/day, also add CDN:
// Popular short URLs served from CDN edge nodes globally
// Reduces latency and DB+Redis load for most popular URLs

// Write path: 1,160/second is fine for single PostgreSQL write primary
// with read replicas. No sharding needed at this scale.

// 6. TRADE-OFFS:
// 'With more time: URL expiration cleanup job (Module 28 pattern);
//  Analytics pipeline to ClickHouse (Module 35); global
//  consistency across regions if going multi-region (Module 29)'

