// S: 'While building and load testing my Module 28 background
//    job system portfolio project at 3am before a demo'
// T: 'Payment confirmation emails were being sent 3-4 times per
//    order - a potential reputation and cost problem'
// A: 'I checked the BullMQ job logs and saw jobs were marked
//    "completed" but the email service was being called before
//    the status update was atomic - a race condition. I added
//    a transaction wrapping the job status update and the email
//    dispatch, making them atomic.'
// R: 'Duplicate sends dropped to 0 in the next 500 load-test
//    jobs. I documented this as an example of why idempotency
//    matters in background job systems in my portfolio README.'

// STORY 2: Evidence-based technical decision
// S: 'During Module 35 exercises, I saw my analytics queries
//    taking 45+ seconds on the OLTP PostgreSQL database'
// T: 'I needed to decide whether to add indexes or move to a
//    separate OLAP store - both would take time to implement'
// A: 'I ran EXPLAIN ANALYZE on the slow queries and found they
//    were full sequential scans across 50M rows, reading 15
//    columns when only 3 were needed. This was a storage layout
//    mismatch, not an indexing problem. I documented this in an
//    ADR and migrated to DuckDB for analytics queries.'
// R: 'Analytics queries dropped from 45s to 1.2s. The ADR
//    documented that the evidence pointed to storage layout,
//    not indexing, which saved hours of unproductive index tuning.'

// STORY 3: Mistake and learning
// S: 'Early in Module 28, my background job system was silently
//    dropping failed jobs'
// T: 'Users were reporting undelivered emails but I couldn't
//    identify why - the system appeared healthy'
// A: 'I eventually discovered I had a missing catch block in
//    the job worker, so failed jobs were dying silently and
//    leaving the status as "processing" forever. I added the
//    catch block AND added a Module 36 alert on jobs stuck
//    in processing for >5 minutes.'
// R: 'No more silent failures. The real lesson: I learned that
//    unhappy paths (failures, errors) need to be explicitly
//    designed for and monitored, not treated as exceptional
//    cases that shouldn't happen.'

